.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAG-COPY-ROUTE 1 "November 2026" "euclid-cli 2.3.2" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eag-copy-route \- create a route from an existing one
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eag copy-route
.BR \-\-route\-id | \-r
.I ROUTE
[\fB\-\-new\-route\-id\fR|\fB\-n\fR \fIID\fR]
[\fIany option update-route takes\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Creates a new route from an existing one. The copy has everything the
source has \- path, methods, backend, authentication, timeout, upload
settings, state \- with the options given here changed exactly as
.BR euclid-cli-eag-update-route (1)
would change them, and is stored under its own route ID. The source is
not touched.
.PP
It is for a route that differs from another in one or two settings: the
same path published in another namespace, another set of methods on the
same path sent to another application, a second version of an API on a
neighbouring path. Only what differs is written down, so nothing that
should have stayed the same can be mistyped on the way.
.SH WHAT HAS TO DIFFER
Two routes may not answer for the same path and the same methods in the
same namespace, so a copy has to differ from its source in at least one
of
.BR \-\-path ,
.B \-\-methods
or
.BR \-\-namespace .
A copy that would collide with its source, or with any other route, is
refused and nothing is stored.
.PP
A route ID is unique within a namespace. Copied into another namespace,
the copy keeps the source's route ID unless
.B \-\-new\-route\-id
names another; within the same namespace,
.B \-\-new\-route\-id
is needed.
.SH CHECKED AS A WHOLE
The copy is checked the way an update is: as the route it will be, not
field by field. In particular:
.IP \(bu 2
an application it names has to exist in the copy's namespace \- which a
route copied into another namespace without
.B \-\-application
keeps from its source, so that application has to be deployed there
too;
.IP \(bu 2
an upload route still needs a bucket, no application, and
authentication other than none.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-route\-id " " \fIROUTE\fR
The route to copy, in the namespace your session is working in.
Required.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-new\-route\-id " " \fIID\fR
The copy's route ID. Defaults to the source's, which only works
together with
.BR \-\-namespace .
.PP
Every other option is one of
.BR euclid-cli-eag-update-route (1)'s,
with the same meaning:
.BR \-\-path ,
.BR \-\-methods ,
.BR \-\-application ,
.BR \-\-module ,
.BR \-\-action ,
.BR \-\-type ,
.BR \-\-bucket ,
.BR \-\-key\-prefix ,
.BR \-\-max\-bytes ,
.BR \-\-part\-size ,
.BR \-\-content\-types ,
.BR \-\-region ,
.BR \-\-namespace ,
.BR \-\-authentication ,
.B \-\-timeout
and
.BR \-\-active .
.PP
.B \-\-active false
creates the copy out of service, to be put in service with
.B update-route \-\-active true
once it has been checked.
.SH OUTPUT
The new route, as JSON, in the form
.BR euclid-cli-eag-get-route (1)
shows it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The copy was created.
.TP
.B 1
No such source route (HTTP 404), a route of the copy's ID exists
already in the copy's namespace or its path and methods are taken (HTTP
409), the application it names does not exist in its namespace (HTTP
404), an option was invalid, or you are not an administrator.
.SH EXAMPLES
The same route, published for integration:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eag copy-route --route-id orders-api --namespace integration
.fi
.RE
.PP
A second version beside the first:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eag copy-route --route-id orders-v1 --new-route-id orders-v2 \e
    --path /api/v2/orders --application orders-v2
.fi
.RE
.PP
Writes on the same path to another application, created out of service:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eag copy-route --route-id orders-read --new-route-id orders-write \e
    --methods POST,PUT,DELETE --application orders-writer --active false
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eag (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eag-update-route (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eag-create-route (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eag-get-route (1)
